Privilege Escalation.


Alert Summary:
A privilege escalation exploit was detected on an internal Windows 10 machine. The malware impersonated a legitimate Windows process to evade detection.

Indicators:

	•	Hostname: Victor
	•	IP Address: 172.16.17.207
	•	Malware: svohost.exe (typosquatting svchost.exe)
	•	File Path: C:\temp\service_installer\svohost.exe
	•	File Hash: b432dcf4a0f0b601b1d79848467137a5e25cab5a0b7b1224be9d3b6540122db9
	•	Parent Process: PowerShell
	•	CVE: CVE-2024-49138
	•	Device Action: Allowed (not quarantined)

Analysis:
The malware svohost.exe was designed to mimic the legitimate Windows process svchost.exe using typosquatting. It was executed from C:\temp\ instead of C:\Windows\System32, which is an immediate red flag. PowerShell was used as the parent process to execute the malware. VirusTotal confirmed 50/71 security vendors flagged the file as malicious, classified as Trojan.barys/expl. No confirmed C2 communication was identified in network logs.

Recommended Actions:
Contain the machine Victor, remove svohost.exe, and patch the system against CVE-2024-49138.
